# Cloudflare Pages response headers for the Hevalo web app (hevalo.app).
# The app is a self-contained Vite SPA: it loads only its own hashed
# /assets/*.js + /assets/*.css and talks to the Render API over HTTPS. The
# policy below allows exactly that. If the API origin changes (VITE_API_URL),
# update connect-src to match; if the app renders R2/CDN media, img-src/media-src
# already allow https:.
#
# connect-src names the API twice, once per scheme, and both are needed. The
# realtime gateway (live quiz matches, chat, presence) is a WebSocket on the
# same host, and an `https://` source does not cover `wss://` — CSP3 lets `ws:`
# and `wss:` sources match `https:`, never the other way round. With only the
# https entry Chrome refused the socket outright ("Refused to connect to
# 'wss://kurda-api.onrender.com/realtime…' … violates connect-src"), so every
# realtime feature fell back to nothing.
#
# style-src carries 'unsafe-inline' because React writes every inline style as
# a style attribute, and this app has 128 of them across 44 files. Without it
# the browser blocks the attribute — not only <style> blocks — and drops the
# declaration silently: no console error unless something is listening for
# securitypolicyviolation. Live, the site reported effectiveDirective
# "style-src-attr", blockedURI "inline".
#
# What that cost, for as long as the app has been deployed: the race and
# Wordle-battle progress bars never moved, the XP level bar never filled, and
# the photo editor's colour picker had no gradient.
#
# script-src stays 'self' with no 'unsafe-inline', which is the directive
# that matters: CSS-only exfiltration needs markup injection first, and that
# is what script-src and the escaping in React already prevent.
#
# style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline' would be tighter, but a browser without
# CSP3 attribute support falls back to style-src and silently reproduces
# exactly this bug, so it is not the safer option it looks like.
#
# microphone=(self) and media-src blob: are for the lesson player's speaking
# exercise: a learner records a sentence (the microphone, on this origin only,
# and only after the browser asks them) and plays their own take back from a
# blob: URL beside the native recording. With microphone=() the browser never
# asked and recording always failed; without blob: the take would not play.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https:; font-src 'self'; media-src 'self' blob: https:; connect-src 'self' https://kurda-api.onrender.com wss://kurda-api.onrender.com; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(self), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin

# The public dictionary (/ferheng/*), which is a different kind of page.
#
# `_headers` rules ADD, they do not replace: a /ferheng/ page matches `/*`
# above as well as this, and is served every header from both. Measured on
# the live site — two Content-Security-Policy headers, and a
# Strict-Transport-Security whose value was its own value twice, comma-joined.
# That last one matters: the preload list wants one well-formed header.
#
# So this block names only what differs, and the shared headers are left to
# `/*`. The second CSP is deliberate and is the exception: a browser given two
# policies enforces BOTH, so the result is their intersection and can only be
# stricter than either. Both allow scripts from self and nothing else — no
# inline script, no other host (see below for why self). Inline style, which
# `/*` permits, this does not.
#
# That is the second of the two things between a stranger's Wiktionary text
# and code running here. The first is the escaping in web/scripts/escape.ts,
# and this one does not depend on that one being right. Verified live: a
# fetch() from one of these pages is blocked before it leaves.
#
# A longer cache than the app, because a word's meaning does not change
# between deploys. Not `immutable`: the next import rewrites it in place.
# `font-src 'self'` is the one thing added since: the typefaces are copied out
# of node_modules into /ferheng/fonts/ at build time and served from this
# origin. A font from a CDN would need a host named here, which would be the
# first third party these pages talk to; a file on the edge costs nothing and
# tells nobody that somebody read a word.
#
# `script-src 'self'` is for chrome.js, which puts the page into the reader's
# own language — the pages exist in two, the app in nine; see
# web/scripts/ferheng-chrome.ts. It does not reopen what the paragraph above
# closes: an injected inline script or handler is still refused, and a
# <script src> can only name a file on this origin, every one of which is ours
# (user uploads live on another host, the Worker's pages are HTML, and
# `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` above stops anything not served as
# JavaScript from running). connect-src is still 'none', so even one of the
# app's own bundles pulled in by an injection could reach nothing.
#
# 'self' rather than chrome.js's full URL, because a URL names a host: pinned
# to https://hevalo.app/ the file was refused on every preview deployment,
# which is where the fix gets checked, and the page stayed English there.
/ferheng/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, s-maxage=86400

# The same dictionary in English. Two sibling paths rather than one nested
# under the other, because everything a level under them is a letter or a range
# of words named from the corpus, and both `ku` and `en` are plausible range
# names — see web/scripts/ferheng-copy.ts. Every header has to be repeated:
# `_headers` matches a path, and /dictionary/ does not match /ferheng/*.
/dictionary/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, s-maxage=86400
